<GuideLayout
  title="Multi day hikes around Australia"
  seoTitle="Multi Day Hikes in Australia: A State-by-State Guide"
  pageImage={guideImage}
  pageImageAlt="Main Range National Park seen from farmland at Rosevale, Queensland"
  description="Best multi day hikes Australia has to offer, state by state: verified routes, bookings and fire rules for NSW, Victoria, Tasmania and overnight Brisbane hikes."
  eyebrow="Where to go"
  readTime="10 min read"
>
  <p>
    Australia's best multi day hikes are spread across very different terrain:
    coastal circuits in Victoria, sandstone valleys west of Sydney, hut-to-hut
    alpine walking in Tasmania, and rainforest ranges behind Brisbane. Picking
    one usually starts with picking a state, then narrowing to a route that
    matches your fitness and how much booking admin you want to deal with.
  </p>
  <p>
    This guide is a starting map, not a track report. Each section below
    summarises what to expect in that state and links to our full regional
    guide, which carries the verified distances, booking links and fire-season
    rules for that area.
  </p>

  <div class="takeaways">
    <strong>Key takeaways</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Victoria, NSW, Tasmania and Queensland each have their own booking
        systems and fire-season rules; check the specific state before you plan
        dates.
      </li>
      <li>
        The <a href="/guides/overnight-hikes-blue-mountains/">Blue Mountains</a> and
        <a href="/guides/overnight-hikes-victoria/">Great Ocean Walk</a> are two of
        the most accessible first multi day hikes near a capital city.
      </li>
      <li>
        Tasmania's Overland Track and Three Capes Track need paid, capped
        bookings that sell out well ahead of season.
      </li>
      <li>
        Near Brisbane, the Scenic Rim Trail and Gold Coast Hinterland Great Walk
        are the two established multi-day options within about two hours' drive.
      </li>
      <li>
        Whichever state you choose, your <a
          href="/guides/overnight-hike-packing-list/">packing list</a
        > and sleep system matter as much as the track itself.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>Table of contents</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="#victoria">Multi day hikes in Victoria</a></li>
    <li><a href="#new-south-wales">Multi day hikes in New South Wales</a></li>
    <li><a href="#tasmania">Multi day hikes in Tasmania</a></li>
    <li>
      <a href="#queensland">Overnight hikes near Brisbane, Queensland</a>
    </li>
    <li><a href="#comparison">How the states compare</a></li>
    <li><a href="#gear">What gear changes state to state?</a></li>
    <li><a href="#bottom-line">The bottom line</a></li>
    <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="victoria">What are the best multi day hikes in Victoria?</h2>
  <p>
    Victoria's two headline multi-day routes both run along the coast rather
    than through alpine country, which makes them a comparatively gentle entry
    point. The Great Ocean Walk links eight hike-in campgrounds from Apollo Bay
    toward the Twelve Apostles, so you can walk one short leg as a first
    overnighter or string several together into a longer trip. Wilsons
    Promontory's Southern Circuit does the same with a chain of coastal
    campsites including Sealers Cove and Refuge Cove, though Parks Victoria
    itself grades the direct Sealers Cove track a challenging Grade 4 walk. That
    direct track is currently closed, so the walk-in via Telegraph Track runs
    about 25km rather than 10km, which makes it suit someone with at least one
    overnight hike behind them rather than a first attempt.
  </p>
  <p>
    Almost every hike-in campsite in Victoria needs an online booking through
    ParkStay Victoria before you leave home, and that booking doubles as your
    camping permit. A handful of exceptions exist, such as Cathedral Range's
    Farmyard camping area, which Parks Victoria lists as needing no booking or
    fee. Fire rules matter too: Victoria's Country Fire Authority runs a Fire
    Danger Period that can restrict or ban campfires and close parks outright on
    the worst days, so check current ratings for your specific route before
    committing to dates.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our <a href="/guides/overnight-hikes-victoria/"
      >overnight hikes Victoria guide</a
    > covers verified one-way distances, booking links and a beginner-suitable route
    table for both the Great Ocean Walk and Wilsons Promontory.
  </p>

  <h2 id="new-south-wales">
    What are the best multi day hikes in New South Wales?
  </h2>
  <p>
    NSW splits neatly into two guides because the Blue Mountains has such a
    dense network of its own. West of Sydney, walk-in NPWS campgrounds like
    Acacia Flat near Blue Gum Forest and Kedumba River Crossing sit within a
    couple of hours of the city, and most need an online booking even where
    there's no nightly camping fee. Perrys Lookdown to Blue Gum Forest is
    commonly recommended as a first overnight hike, a short return walk with a
    steep, graded-hard descent that feels harder again with a full pack.
  </p>
  <p>
    Outside the Blue Mountains, NSW has multi-day options from a two-day coastal
    walk in Royal National Park through to multi-week, section-hiked
    long-distance tracks. Almost every NPWS campsite in the state, including
    free ones, needs an online booking, and for remote country with no bookable
    site, NPWS asks hikers to lodge a free Trip Intention Form instead. NSW's
    statewide Bush Fire Danger Period runs from 1 October to 31 March, and rules
    on stoves and open fires tighten further on a declared Total Fire Ban day.
  </p>
  <p>
    See our <a href="/guides/overnight-hikes-blue-mountains/"
      >overnight hikes Blue Mountains guide</a
    > for that region's verified routes and fire rules, and our <a
      href="/guides/multi-day-hikes-nsw/">multi day hikes NSW guide</a
    > for the rest of the state, including the Coast Track, Yuraygir Coastal Walk
    and the Green Gully Track.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tasmania">What are the best multi day hikes in Tasmania?</h2>
  <p>
    Tasmania has arguably Australia's best-known multi-day walking, and also its
    least predictable weather. The Overland Track runs 65km between Cradle
    Mountain and Lake St Clair over about six days and needs a paid booking
    during its 1 October to 31 May season; Parks and Wildlife Service Tasmania
    describes it as a walk for fit, experienced walkers rather than a first
    attempt. The Three Capes Track is a more beginner-friendly booked option:
    48km over 4 days and 3 nights with hut accommodation included in the fee, so
    you're not carrying a tent.
  </p>
  <p>
    For something with no booking system at all, the Freycinet Peninsula Circuit
    needs no reservation and has no camping fee, though it is a fuel-stove-only
    area with limited, unreliable water. Whichever walk you pick, Tasmanian
    Government safety guidance is blunt that alpine weather can turn from mild
    to severe within minutes at any time of year, so pack for conditions well
    outside what the calendar suggests and check the Bureau of Meteorology
    forecast close to departure.
  </p>
  <p>
    Full booking details, fees and a route comparison table are in our <a
      href="/guides/multi-day-hikes-tasmania/">multi day hikes Tasmania guide</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2 id="queensland">
    What are the best overnight hikes near Brisbane, Queensland?
  </h2>
  <figure class="guide-photo">
    <Image
      src={guideImage}
      alt="Main Range National Park seen from farmland at Rosevale, Queensland"
      widths={[360, 720, 1024, 1400]}
      sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 32px), 680px"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>
      Photo: <a
        href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Main_Range_from_Rosevale.jpg"
        rel="noopener">Main Range from Rosevale</a
      > by Shiftchange, public domain (<a
        href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/"
        rel="noopener">CC0</a
      >), via Wikimedia Commons.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Brisbane doesn't yet have a dedicated Bush Gums region guide, but two
    established multi-day routes sit within about two hours' drive and are both
    run through Queensland's Department of the Environment, Tourism, Science and
    Innovation (Queensland Parks and Wildlife Service). The <a
      href="https://parks.qld.gov.au/parks/main-range/about/scenic-rim-trail"
      rel="noopener">Scenic Rim Trail</a
    > in Main Range National Park is a 47km, four-day walk for experienced bushwalkers,
    according to Queensland Parks. It runs from Thornton View to Cunninghams Gap,
    walked one direction only, north to south. Camping is sold as a single 3-night
    package covering all three walkers' camps along the route, with a maximum group
    size of four people and a one-night limit at each camp; you book through the <a
      href="https://book.parks.qld.gov.au/scenic-rim-trail"
      rel="noopener">Queensland National Parks Booking Service</a
    >. Main Range National Park sits roughly 90 minutes to two hours from
    Brisbane via the Cunningham Highway.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a
      href="https://parks.qld.gov.au/parks/great-walks-gold-coast-hinterland/about"
      rel="noopener">Gold Coast Hinterland Great Walk</a
    > is the other main option: a 54km, three-day walk linking Lamington and Springbrook
    national parks, recommended west to east from O'Reilly's in Lamington to The Settlement
    camping area in Springbrook. Camping at the trail's own <a
      href="https://parks.qld.gov.au/parks/great-walks-gold-coast-hinterland/camping"
      rel="noopener">Woonoongoora walkers' camp</a
    > is booked through the Queensland National Parks Booking Service, but bookings
    differ by campsite along the route: O'Reilly's and Binna Burra are privately run
    lodges with their own separate booking systems, not the government service. Check
    the official <a
      href="https://parks.qld.gov.au/parks/great-walks-gold-coast-hinterland/camping"
      rel="noopener">Gold Coast Hinterland Great Walk camping page</a
    > for the current booking link and fee for each night of your route.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because these two walks aren't yet covered by a dedicated Bush Gums guide,
    treat the details above as a starting point and always check the linked
    Queensland Parks page for current fees, closures and permit numbers before
    you book.
  </p>

